// axverity-working2.axreg — §5b composites for the AXVERITY_WRITE_PATH_SLICE_V1
// vertical. Every fn is kind leaf; bodies resolve via --lib-dir lib_coreir at
// link time (FNREF_COMPOSITE_RESOLVER_v0.1). identity = sha256(utf8 name),
// nothing else.
// Bridge (Rust-resident) fns belong in axis-bridge.axreg, not here.
// Types (Unit/Int/Text/Bool/Bytes/Value/TextList) are declared in
// axis-bridge.axreg, co-fed with this registry by every invocation — do not
// redeclare them here.
//
// The 4 src/*.m1 --entries outers (hot_mem_write, mem_controller,
// disk_controller, flusher) do NOT get fn entries here — --entries binaries
// are resolved by file-stem at bridge link time, not by registry identity
// (verified: graphcore.axreg has no entry for gcore_serve/gcore_flush_worker/
// gcore_pool_allocator/gcore_timer either).

registry axverity-working2 0.1

// ── Channel topology (CHANNELS_STATIC) ──────────────────────────────────────
// mem_controller creates blocks, tracks them, and is the ONLY thing that
// ever talks to disk_controller — sealing, ejection-triggering, all of
// it. What it does NOT do is touch a block's own content (payload or
// footer): it plans a block's footer content in advance (flag/next-seq,
// computable purely from N and that block's own index, both known up
// front) and hands the whole write bundle to hot_mem_write in one go.
// hot_mem_write is the sole content writer — payload, footer, AND the
// block's status marker — and reports back nothing except a bare
// completion signal (wp-seal-done); mem_controller already knows the
// block's address (it remembers what it assigned, via its own
// self-mailbox) and derives the length itself (from its own seq * the
// payload capacity it decided, against the input length it staged), so
// there is nothing left for hot_mem_write to report. mem_controller waits
// on that signal before forwarding to disk_controller — that's the one
// piece of real synchronization in the whole pipeline, and it exists
// purely so a block never gets forwarded for ejection before its write
// has actually finished. flusher is the sole content reader, and resets
// the block's status marker once ejected.
//
// Each physical block also gets its own status cell (cell_new_raw), an
// atomic Int: 0 = not in use, 1 = writing, 2 = ready to read. mem_controller
// stamps 0->1 at assignment time (pure bookkeeping — "this slot is now
// spoken for", not touching content); hot_mem_write stamps 1->2
// (cell_cas_raw) once its writes are done; flusher stamps 2->0 once it has
// ejected. The cell address travels alongside its block's ptr everywhere,
// the same way (ptr, len) do — no separate ptr-to-entry lookup table.
//
// Every channel below carries an address, an offset/length, planning data
// (flag/next-seq), or a bare signal — never a block's actual bytes.
//
// Every group of related values below (an address + its status cell, a
// full block assignment, etc.) travels as ONE list-valued message on ONE
// channel (list_of_2/list_of_3/list_append — verified empirically through
// the real toolchain, see scratchpad probe), not as N separate channels
// read back-to-back. Each channel op is a Mutex-guarded VecDeque
// operation; N separate takes for one logical unit is N lock/unlock
// cycles for something that's really one event, and this is the most
// time-sensitive layer in the pipeline. Building the list locally
// (list_of_3/list_append) costs a few plain Vec pushes, no
// synchronization at all — genuinely cheaper, not just tidier.

// hot_mem_write stages the whole input into raw memory ITSELF at its own
// startup (one fs_read_bytes + one mem_write_raw — no channel hop for the
// bytes at all, since this is time-sensitive and every extra copy costs
// real work). wp-input-ptr is hot_mem_write's own self-mailbox — nothing
// else ever reads it, since only hot_mem_write's own step ever
// dereferences the staged input. wp-input-len is the one piece
// mem_controller actually needs (to derive each sealed block's length
// from its own seq * payload-capacity arithmetic) — sent once by
// hot_mem_write, read via take+put-back "peek" by both hot_mem_write's
// own step and mem_controller's step. Either way, only a plain address or
// a plain length ever crosses a channel here — never the bytes
// themselves, and no encoding of either value beyond what they already
// are (raw Ints).
channel wp-input-ptr
  type Value
end

channel wp-input-len
  type Value
end

// flusher -> mem_controller: the pool of (block address, status-cell
// address) pairs free for reuse — one list_of_2 message per pair.
// Pre-seeded with the 2 spare blocks' pairs by mem_controller at startup
// (the first block goes straight to hot_mem_write, same as before).
channel wp-free-pool
  type Value
end

// mem_controller -> hot_mem_write: everything hot_mem_write needs to
// write one block, decided entirely in advance and handed over as ONE
// list_of_5 message — [ptr, cap, status, flag, nextseq]. No sequence
// number is included: hot_mem_write has no use for it — only
// mem_controller cares about ejection ordering, and it already tracks
// that as its own loop accumulator.
channel wp-active-block
  type Value
end

// mem_controller's own self-mailbox: which (block address, status-cell
// address) it most recently assigned as active — one list_of_2 message.
// Recalled at the start of each step to know what it's sealing —
// hot_mem_write never reports this back, mem_controller already decided
// it.
channel wp-mc-active
  type Value
end

// hot_mem_write -> mem_controller: a bare completion signal, nothing
// else. mem_controller can't safely forward a block to disk_controller
// until the write into it has actually finished, so this is purely a
// synchronization barrier — not a data handoff.
channel wp-seal-done
  type Value
end

// mem_controller -> disk_controller: mem_controller is the only thing
// that ever sends here — one list_of_4 message, [ptr, len, seq, status].
// It never touched the block's content, but it derived the length itself
// and recalled the address/status from its own self-mailbox.
channel wp-write
  type Value
end

// disk_controller -> flusher: same passthrough one hop further, same
// [ptr, len, seq, status] shape. flusher is where the address finally
// gets dereferenced (mem_read_raw) and the resulting bytes get written to
// disk — as late as possible.
channel wp-flush
  type Value
end

// AXVERITY_WATERMARK_ACK_V1 — flusher's own self-mailbox for the durable-
// watermark cell handle it minted at startup: wp_flusher_step takes it
// and immediately puts it back every iteration (the same take+put-back
// idiom wp-mc-active already uses), since loop_count's step fns take
// exactly one Int accumulator and get everything else via a channel.
channel wp-durable-cell
  type Value
end

// AXVERITY_WATERMARK_ACK_V1 — one-shot handoff of the same cell handle to
// ack_bench, read once at its own startup. Deliberately a separate
// channel from wp-durable-cell: two independent take+put-back peekers on
// the same bounded channel would contend/race for no reason.
channel wp-durable-cell-pub
  type Value
end

// AXVERITY_WATERMARK_ACK_V1 — ack_bench's own self-mailbox, mirroring
// wp-durable-cell's role but for wp_ack_bench_step's per-iteration reads.
channel wp-ackbench-cell
  type Value
end

// AXVERITY_WATERMARK_ACK_V1 — flusher's durable-advance notification, via
// bchan_send/bchan_take (bounded/blocking) — NOT channel_send/
// event_subscribe/wait, since an M1-authored fn can't be used as wait()'s
// Fn handler (see wp_wait_durable.m1's header). One bchan_take per
// wp_wait_durable_step iteration blocks for the next notification; the
// watermark cell is the actual source of truth, so consuming a
// notification meant for a different target is harmless — only ever an
// extra recheck, never incorrect (see wp_wait_durable.m1).
channel wp-durable-advance
  type Value
end

// ── Composites (lib/*.m1) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH step 1: was `effect pure` + `deterministic true`
// while its body was the literal Int(16384). The body now reads
// .axverity-store/config/block_capacity, so the declaration has to follow —
// declaring a file read as pure is precisely the hazard CLAUDE.md section 2
// names (CCall CSE hash-consing merges identical pure+deterministic calls).
// Losing the dedup costs one config-file read per block, against a per-block
// fsync measured at 9.005 ms.
fn wp_block_capacity
  identity 0xdd68cf0fb899e40c25c4c7740b79e9ddbb6e79bd6a26d00652e8ba736d70609d
  kind leaf
  in (Unit)
  out Int
  effect fullIo
  deterministic false
  idempotent true
end

